From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 16:33:01 +0200
Subject: PCI: intel-gw: add xrx500 (GRX500/GRX350) root complex support

The Lantiq/Intel xRX500 family puts three of these root complexes on one
SoC. The application block is the same one this driver already knows --
CCR with LTSSM in bit 0 at 0x10, MSG_CR at 0x30, PMC at 0x44, IRNEN at
0xf4, IRNCR at 0xf8, INTx starting at bit 12 -- and the port logic
registers behind it are stock DesignWare. The vendor's out-of-tree
driver is this driver with the macros renamed; its link-up check is
dw_pcie_link_up() spelled out longhand. What genuinely differs is the
byte order, the absence of an address translation unit in the
configuration path, and a couple of resources this SoC does not have.

Byte order first, because it is the part that fails silently.

This is a big-endian MIPS platform, and INTEL_MIPS deliberately does not
select SWAP_IO_SPACE -- that switch is global, and the SoC's working
ethernet datapath is written against the unswapped accessors. The
controller hands back numerically correct 32-bit dwords regardless, so
readl() is right and readb()/readw() read the mirrored byte lane. Left
alone, dw_pcie_setup_rc()'s 16-bit write of PCI_CLASS_DEVICE lands on
the revision ID and programming interface bytes instead of the class
code. The root port then does not describe itself as a bridge, the core
never scans the secondary bus, and there are no endpoints and no
register that looks wrong. dw_pcie_find_capability()'s walk over the
capability list is byte- and word-sized as well, and goes the same way.

So provide .read_dbi/.write_dbi that do every sub-dword access as a
dword read plus shift, and a dword read-modify-write. That covers all of
DBI, and the iATU and eDMA blocks, which reuse the same callbacks with a
different base -- hence honouring the @base argument rather than
pci->dbi_base. It does not cover the root bus: dw_pcie_ops installs
pci_generic_config_read()/write(), which reach dbi_base directly with
readb()/readw(). Substituting the _read32/_write32 variants fixes that
half; they read and write whole dwords and extract the lane themselves.
This is the shape pcie-bt1.c uses for the same reason.

Second, configuration space. The controller decodes its 8 MiB "config"
window in hardware -- three bits of bus, five of device, three of
function -- with no iATU in the path, which is what the vendor
devicetree means by "intel,iatu = <0>". Supplying .child_ops is how that
gets communicated: dw_pcie_setup_rc() programs the iATU only while
child_ops is still the DesignWare default, so installing our own turns
the whole iATU path off structurally rather than by a flag. Three bits
of bus number is also why the binding stops the bus-range at 7; bus 8
would alias back onto DBI.

For the same reason the generic ECAM path has to be declined explicitly,
or it would leave bridge->sysdata pointing at a struct pci_config_window
that our .map_bus reads back as a struct dw_pcie_rp. That is not a
theoretical tidy-up. Of the two tests in dw_pcie_ecam_enabled(), the
window-size one already passes here: an 8 MiB config window is 8 ECAM
buses and bus-range = <0 7> asks for exactly 8. Only the 256 MiB
alignment test still says no, and it does so because of where these
windows happen to sit rather than because anything in the binding pins
them there. The decision is taken before ->init runs, so it goes through
the soc data like the rest.

The child accessor also refuses every device but 0 on the first child
bus, which dw_pcie_other_conf_map_bus() leaves to only_one_child() in
the core. That helper is not equivalent here. It answers yes only once
the parent has been recognised as PCI_EXP_TYPE_ROOT_PORT, which is a
conclusion drawn from the PCIe capability walk and the class code --
that is, from the very sub-dword DBI accessors described above. Should
those be subtly wrong, the guard is what makes the failure legible: the
symptom becomes a link that enumerates nothing, rather than thirty-two
identical phantom endpoints on bus 1 with BARs assigned to each out of
the host bridge's memory window. The vendor driver has the same check.

The root complex' own BAR0 and BAR1 read as zero, as they do in the
vendor driver ("NB, ignore RC BAR0/BAR1 due to hardware bugs"), so the
core sizes them as absent. There is deliberately no matching guard on
writes -- the vendor has none either, and it is harmless: __pci_read_base()
writes all-ones, reads the size back, and restores the value it first
read, which this quirk made zero, and which is what dw_pcie_setup_rc()
leaves in BAR0 anyway.

Third, the chiptop byte swappers. Each root complex has an inbound and
an outbound swap bit in a syscon register outside the controller, named
by "intel,syscon" with the bit numbers in "intel,inbound-shift" and
"intel,outbound-shift" and the per-board policy in the matching "-swap"
properties. Missing shifts are refused rather than defaulted, and out of
range shifts with them: the vendor BSP treats absent properties as zero
and writes bit 0 twice, which on a three-RC SoC means quietly swapping
some other port's traffic, and a shift past 31 would land back on bit 0
by way of the shift count masking rather than by way of the zero. The
regmap itself is mandatory on this SoC, and the soc data says so: the
lookup helper returns a plain NULL both for an absent property and for
a kernel built without MFD_SYSCON, and a NULL regmap means the swappers
keep whatever the bootloader left them at. That is invisible from every
register -- the link trains, configuration space reads correctly, BARs
get assigned -- and shows up only as inbound DMA payloads with their
bytes mirrored, which is to say as a device firmware load that fails or
a descriptor ring of nonsense, diagnosed a long way from here. Failing
the probe is the cheaper outcome. The write happens at the top of
intel_pcie_host_setup(), before the core
comes out of reset and before the link is trained. That is also the fix
for a vendor bug: it does this once in probe and never again, so the
setting does not survive suspend/resume; going through host_setup()
covers ->init and intel_pcie_resume_noirq() alike.

Fourth, interrupts. INTx only for now. INTA-D reach the GIC on their own
lines through the devicetree "interrupt-map", and there is no MSI
support here yet, so the soc data unmasks the four INTx sources and
nothing else. The rest of PCIE_APP_IRN_INT are latched status bits in
IRNCR, and this driver neither requests the application block interrupt
nor clears that register; enabling them would strand a level line
asserted for whoever requests that interrupt next.

Keeping the DesignWare built-in MSI controller out needs a positive act,
because dw_pcie_host_init() derives pp->has_msi_ctrl from the absence of
an .msi_init hook and of "msi-parent"/"msi-map". Supplying a hook that
returns zero clears has_msi_ctrl, which makes dw_pcie_msi_host_init()
and the MSI register programming in dw_pcie_msi_init() both no-ops, and
satisfies the one call site left. Supplying nothing would be actively
worse than doing nothing: dw_pcie_msi_host_init() falls back to
platform_get_irq(pdev, 0) when it finds no "msi" interrupt, which on
this binding is the application block's "ir" line, and would take it
over as a chained MSI demultiplexer.

Lastly the resources. This SoC has no core reset line of its own -- the
vendor devicetree describes no "resets" here -- so the soc data marks it
optional. Clock, phy and PERST# gpio are all present and stay mandatory.
The devicetree also needs no "pcie" alias: the vendor driver's single
of_alias_get_id() call feeds nothing but debug prints and an interrupt
name string, while the register bit positions come from the explicit
shift properties and the PCI domain from "linux,pci-domain".

Two pieces of the vendor bring-up sequence are intentionally not
carried over. Its RC-mode and lane/width setup are gated on the link
capability register reporting Gen3 or better, and this silicon reports
Gen2, so on this hardware they have never run; making them run would be
a change to behaviour, not a port of it. Its L2 handshake on teardown is
gated the same way, and this driver already reaches the same conclusion
from max-link-speed = <2>.

pci->atu_base stays at dbi_base + 0xC0000. dw_pcie_get_resources() would
otherwise leave it at the dbi_base + 0x300000 default, three megabytes
past the end of the one-megabyte "dbi" mapping.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-intel-gw.c b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-intel-gw.c
index 5d34ca9ba069..9be70d5fc536 100644
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-intel-gw.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-intel-gw.c
@@ -9,11 +9,13 @@
 #include <linux/clk.h>
 #include <linux/gpio/consumer.h>
 #include <linux/iopoll.h>
+#include <linux/mfd/syscon.h>
 #include <linux/mod_devicetable.h>
 #include <linux/pci_regs.h>
 #include <linux/phy/phy.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
 #include <linux/property.h>
+#include <linux/regmap.h>
 #include <linux/reset.h>
 
 #include "../../pci.h"
@@ -49,13 +51,30 @@
 #define PCIE_APP_IRN_SYS_ERR_RC		BIT(29)
 #define PCIE_APP_INTX_OFST		12
 
+#define PCIE_APP_IRN_INTX \
+	(PCIE_APP_IRN_INTA | PCIE_APP_IRN_INTB | \
+	PCIE_APP_IRN_INTC | PCIE_APP_IRN_INTD)
+
 #define PCIE_APP_IRN_INT \
 	(PCIE_APP_IRN_AER_REPORT | PCIE_APP_IRN_PME | \
 	PCIE_APP_IRN_RX_VDM_MSG | PCIE_APP_IRN_SYS_ERR_RC | \
 	PCIE_APP_IRN_PM_TO_ACK | PCIE_APP_IRN_MSG_LTR | \
 	PCIE_APP_IRN_BW_MGT | PCIE_APP_IRN_LINK_AUTO_BW_STAT | \
-	PCIE_APP_IRN_INTA | PCIE_APP_IRN_INTB | \
-	PCIE_APP_IRN_INTC | PCIE_APP_IRN_INTD)
+	PCIE_APP_IRN_INTX)
+
+/*
+ * Byte-swap control for the three xRX500 root complexes, in the chiptop
+ * syscon rather than in the controller itself. One bit per direction per
+ * RC; which bits belong to which RC is described by "intel,inbound-shift"
+ * and "intel,outbound-shift" in the devicetree.
+ */
+#define PCIE_CHIPTOP_ENDIAN		0x4c
+
+/* xRX500 decodes its 8 MiB config window with only three bits of bus number */
+#define PCIE_XRX500_CFG_BUS(x)		(((x) & 0x7) << 20)
+#define PCIE_XRX500_CFG_DEV(x)		(((x) & 0x1f) << 15)
+#define PCIE_XRX500_CFG_FUNC(x)		(((x) & 0x7) << 12)
+#define PCIE_XRX500_CFG_REG(x)		((x) & 0xffc)
 
 #define RESET_INTERVAL_MS		100
 
@@ -65,12 +84,17 @@
  * @host_ops: DesignWare host bridge hooks for this SoC
  * @irn_mask: Application logic interrupts to unmask once the link is up
  * @core_rst_optional: SoC has no core reset line of its own
+ * @native_ecam: Glue decodes the config window itself, keep generic ECAM away
+ * @needs_endian_syscon: Byte swapping lives outside the controller, "intel,syscon"
+ *			 is not optional here
  */
 struct intel_pcie_soc {
 	const struct dw_pcie_ops	*dw_pcie_ops;
 	const struct dw_pcie_host_ops	*host_ops;
 	u32				irn_mask;
 	bool				core_rst_optional;
+	bool				native_ecam;
+	bool				needs_endian_syscon;
 };
 
 struct intel_pcie {
@@ -82,6 +106,11 @@ struct intel_pcie {
 	struct clk		*core_clk;
 	struct reset_control	*core_rst;
 	struct phy		*phy;
+	struct regmap		*syscon;
+	u32			inbound_shift;
+	u32			outbound_shift;
+	u32			inbound_swap;
+	u32			outbound_swap;
 };
 
 static void pcie_update_bits(void __iomem *base, u32 ofs, u32 mask, u32 val)
@@ -122,6 +151,137 @@ static void pcie_rc_cfg_wr_mask(struct intel_pcie *pcie, u32 ofs,
 	pcie_update_bits(pcie->pci.dbi_base, ofs, mask, val);
 }
 
+/*
+ * The xRX500 answers DBI and configuration reads with a numerically correct
+ * value in the wrong byte order, so every access through this window has to
+ * be swapped in software. The quirk is per-SoC, not per-board.
+ */
+static u32 intel_pcie_xrx500_read_dbi(struct dw_pcie *pci, void __iomem *base,
+				      u32 reg, size_t size)
+{
+	u32 val = readl(base + (reg & ~0x3));
+
+	if (size == 4)
+		return val;
+
+	val >>= (reg & 0x3) * BITS_PER_BYTE;
+
+	return val & (BIT(size * BITS_PER_BYTE) - 1);
+}
+
+static void intel_pcie_xrx500_write_dbi(struct dw_pcie *pci, void __iomem *base,
+					u32 reg, size_t size, u32 val)
+{
+	void __iomem *addr = base + (reg & ~0x3);
+	u32 mask, shift;
+
+	if (size == 4) {
+		writel(val, addr);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	shift = (reg & 0x3) * BITS_PER_BYTE;
+	mask = (BIT(size * BITS_PER_BYTE) - 1) << shift;
+
+	writel((readl(addr) & ~mask) | ((val << shift) & mask), addr);
+}
+
+static const struct dw_pcie_ops intel_pcie_xrx500_ops = {
+	.read_dbi	= intel_pcie_xrx500_read_dbi,
+	.write_dbi	= intel_pcie_xrx500_write_dbi,
+};
+
+/*
+ * The root bus does not go through .read_dbi/.write_dbi -- dw_pcie_ops
+ * installs pci_generic_config_read()/write(), which reach dbi_base with a
+ * bare readb()/readw() and land in the same trap. The 32-bit variants read
+ * and write whole dwords and do the lane extraction themselves, which is
+ * exactly what this controller needs.
+ */
+static int intel_pcie_xrx500_rd_own_conf(struct pci_bus *bus, unsigned int devfn,
+					 int where, int size, u32 *val)
+{
+	/* RC BAR0 and BAR1 are not usable on this controller. */
+	if ((where & ~0x3) == PCI_BASE_ADDRESS_0 ||
+	    (where & ~0x3) == PCI_BASE_ADDRESS_1) {
+		*val = 0;
+		return PCIBIOS_SUCCESSFUL;
+	}
+
+	return pci_generic_config_read32(bus, devfn, where, size, val);
+}
+
+static struct pci_ops intel_pcie_xrx500_own_ops = {
+	.map_bus	= dw_pcie_own_conf_map_bus,
+	.read		= intel_pcie_xrx500_rd_own_conf,
+	.write		= pci_generic_config_write32,
+};
+
+static void __iomem *intel_pcie_xrx500_child_map_bus(struct pci_bus *bus,
+						     unsigned int devfn,
+						     int where)
+{
+	struct dw_pcie_rp *pp = bus->sysdata;
+
+	/* A point-to-point link has exactly one device on it. */
+	if (pci_is_root_bus(bus->parent) && PCI_SLOT(devfn) > 0)
+		return NULL;
+
+	if (!dw_pcie_link_up(to_dw_pcie_from_pp(pp)))
+		return NULL;
+
+	return pp->va_cfg0_base + (PCIE_XRX500_CFG_BUS(bus->number) |
+				   PCIE_XRX500_CFG_DEV(PCI_SLOT(devfn)) |
+				   PCIE_XRX500_CFG_FUNC(PCI_FUNC(devfn)) |
+				   PCIE_XRX500_CFG_REG(where));
+}
+
+/*
+ * This controller decodes the "config" window itself; there is no address
+ * translation unit in the path, which is why the vendor devicetree says
+ * "intel,iatu = <0>". Providing .child_ops at all is what tells
+ * dw_pcie_setup_rc() so: it programs the iATU only when child_ops is still
+ * the DesignWare default. The eight bus numbers the window decodes are also
+ * why the devicetree bus-range must stop at 7 -- bus 8 would alias onto DBI.
+ */
+static struct pci_ops intel_pcie_xrx500_child_ops = {
+	.map_bus	= intel_pcie_xrx500_child_map_bus,
+	.read		= pci_generic_config_read32,
+	.write		= pci_generic_config_write32,
+};
+
+static void intel_pcie_endian_setup(struct intel_pcie *pcie)
+{
+	struct device *dev = pcie->pci.dev;
+	u32 mask, val = 0;
+
+	if (!pcie->syscon)
+		return;
+
+	mask = BIT(pcie->inbound_shift) | BIT(pcie->outbound_shift);
+
+	/*
+	 * The chiptop swappers exist for a big-endian CPU; the vendor BSP
+	 * compiles the "set" side out on little-endian and clears both bits
+	 * unconditionally there. Keep that, but as a plain condition so both
+	 * arms stay compile-checked.
+	 */
+	if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_CPU_BIG_ENDIAN)) {
+		if (pcie->inbound_swap)
+			val |= BIT(pcie->inbound_shift);
+		if (pcie->outbound_swap)
+			val |= BIT(pcie->outbound_shift);
+	}
+
+	regmap_update_bits(pcie->syscon, PCIE_CHIPTOP_ENDIAN, mask, val);
+
+	if (!regmap_read(pcie->syscon, PCIE_CHIPTOP_ENDIAN, &val))
+		dev_info(dev, "chiptop endian %#04x: %#010x (in bit %u swap %u, out bit %u swap %u)\n",
+			 PCIE_CHIPTOP_ENDIAN, val, pcie->inbound_shift,
+			 pcie->inbound_swap, pcie->outbound_shift,
+			 pcie->outbound_swap);
+}
+
 static void intel_pcie_ltssm_enable(struct intel_pcie *pcie)
 {
 	pcie_app_wr_mask(pcie, PCIE_APP_CCR, PCIE_APP_CCR_LTSSM_ENABLE,
@@ -261,6 +421,51 @@ static int intel_pcie_get_resources(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		return ret;
 	}
 
+	pcie->syscon = syscon_regmap_lookup_by_phandle_optional(dev->of_node,
+								"intel,syscon");
+	if (IS_ERR(pcie->syscon))
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(pcie->syscon),
+				     "Failed to look up \"intel,syscon\"\n");
+
+	/*
+	 * syscon_regmap_lookup_by_phandle_optional() returns NULL both when
+	 * the property is absent and when the lookup fails, so the two cases
+	 * have to be told apart by checking for the property first.
+	 */
+	if (pcie->soc->needs_endian_syscon && !pcie->syscon)
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, -EINVAL,
+				     "\"intel,syscon\" is required on this SoC\n");
+
+	if (pcie->syscon) {
+		/*
+		 * Which bits of the chiptop register belong to this root
+		 * complex is not derivable from anything else the node
+		 * carries, and getting it wrong means silently swapping some
+		 * other RC's traffic, so refuse to guess. The vendor BSP
+		 * defaults both to zero and writes bit 0 twice when the
+		 * properties are missing.
+		 */
+		ret = device_property_read_u32(dev, "intel,inbound-shift",
+					       &pcie->inbound_shift);
+		if (!ret)
+			ret = device_property_read_u32(dev, "intel,outbound-shift",
+						       &pcie->outbound_shift);
+		if (ret)
+			return dev_err_probe(dev, ret,
+					     "\"intel,syscon\" needs intel,inbound-shift and intel,outbound-shift\n");
+
+		if (pcie->inbound_shift > 31 || pcie->outbound_shift > 31)
+			return dev_err_probe(dev, -ERANGE,
+					     "Endian bit %u/%u is outside the chiptop register\n",
+					     pcie->inbound_shift,
+					     pcie->outbound_shift);
+
+		device_property_read_u32(dev, "intel,inbound-swap",
+					 &pcie->inbound_swap);
+		device_property_read_u32(dev, "intel,outbound-swap",
+					 &pcie->outbound_swap);
+	}
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
@@ -302,6 +507,17 @@ static int intel_pcie_host_setup(struct intel_pcie *pcie)
 	int ret;
 	struct dw_pcie *pci = &pcie->pci;
 
+	/*
+	 * Configure the chiptop byte swappers before anything else touches the
+	 * controller: they sit in the path the DBI and configuration windows
+	 * ride on, and the syscon block they live in is reachable while the
+	 * core is still in reset and its clock still gated. The vendor BSP
+	 * does this once in probe and never again, so its setting does not
+	 * survive a suspend/resume cycle; doing it here covers ->init and
+	 * intel_pcie_resume_noirq() alike.
+	 */
+	intel_pcie_endian_setup(pcie);
+
 	intel_pcie_core_rst_assert(pcie);
 	intel_pcie_device_rst_assert(pcie);
 	intel_pcie_core_rst_deassert(pcie);
@@ -336,6 +552,12 @@ static int intel_pcie_host_setup(struct intel_pcie *pcie)
 	if (ret)
 		goto err;
 
+	/*
+	 * Clear any pending status before unmasking, as the vendor driver
+	 * does per pin.
+	 */
+	pcie_app_wr(pcie, PCIE_APP_IRNCR, pcie->soc->irn_mask);
+
 	/* Enable integrated interrupts */
 	pcie_app_wr_mask(pcie, PCIE_APP_IRNEN, pcie->soc->irn_mask,
 			 pcie->soc->irn_mask);
@@ -407,12 +629,58 @@ static const struct dw_pcie_host_ops intel_pcie_dw_ops = {
 	.init = intel_pcie_rc_init,
 };
 
+/*
+ * INTx only: INTA-D reach the GIC over their own lines, and there is no MSI
+ * controller behind this root complex.
+ */
+static int intel_pcie_xrx500_msi_init(struct dw_pcie_rp *pp)
+{
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int intel_pcie_xrx500_rc_init(struct dw_pcie_rp *pp)
+{
+	pp->bridge->ops = &intel_pcie_xrx500_own_ops;
+	pp->bridge->child_ops = &intel_pcie_xrx500_child_ops;
+
+	return intel_pcie_rc_init(pp);
+}
+
+static const struct dw_pcie_host_ops intel_pcie_xrx500_dw_ops = {
+	.init = intel_pcie_xrx500_rc_init,
+	.msi_init = intel_pcie_xrx500_msi_init,
+};
+
 static const struct intel_pcie_soc lgm_pcie_soc = {
 	.dw_pcie_ops		= &intel_pcie_ops,
 	.host_ops		= &intel_pcie_dw_ops,
 	.irn_mask		= PCIE_APP_IRN_INT,
 };
 
+static const struct intel_pcie_soc xrx500_pcie_soc = {
+	.dw_pcie_ops		= &intel_pcie_xrx500_ops,
+	.host_ops		= &intel_pcie_xrx500_dw_ops,
+	/* Unmask the INTx sources and nothing else. */
+	.irn_mask		= PCIE_APP_IRN_INTX,
+	/*
+	 * No core reset of its own: the controller leaves reset with the block
+	 * it sits in, and the vendor devicetree describes no "resets" here.
+	 */
+	.core_rst_optional	= true,
+	/*
+	 * The controller decodes the "config" window itself; it is not
+	 * ECAM-shaped, so the bus number is masked to three bits and
+	 * bus-range stops at 7.
+	 */
+	.native_ecam		= true,
+	/*
+	 * Byte swapping for this SoC is in the chiptop syscon rather than in
+	 * the controller, so an absent regmap is not a missing nicety, it is a
+	 * datapath left in an unknown endianness. See intel_pcie_get_resources().
+	 */
+	.needs_endian_syscon	= true,
+};
+
 static int intel_pcie_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
 	struct device *dev = &pdev->dev;
@@ -445,6 +713,7 @@ static int intel_pcie_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 
 	pci->ops = pcie->soc->dw_pcie_ops;
 	pp->ops = pcie->soc->host_ops;
+	pp->native_ecam = pcie->soc->native_ecam;
 
 	ret = dw_pcie_host_init(pp);
 	if (ret) {
@@ -462,6 +731,7 @@ static const struct dev_pm_ops intel_pcie_pm_ops = {
 
 static const struct of_device_id of_intel_pcie_match[] = {
 	{ .compatible = "intel,lgm-pcie", .data = &lgm_pcie_soc },
+	{ .compatible = "intel,xrx500-pcie", .data = &xrx500_pcie_soc },
 	{}
 };
 
